[{:db/id "q-124215/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "d2780eb4-baae-5d12-ad6e-a33c16260410" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124215/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "aa901820-17e5-5f99-b212-f69bcde5bd61" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124215/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "98eddfe0-2875-5074-ae7a-281b12b5a90d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124215/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "ae256e88-a886-5741-ac54-aa2b96597d27" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124215/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "2ab433b9-7d1b-5165-bc11-58812e6030e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124215/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "abb7c224-8364-5b78-9a32-3dc81a0265b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124215/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-124215/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-124215/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-124215/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-124215/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-124215/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124215" :question/id #uuid "1c96229b-5c63-538e-82d5-2f8635304c00" :question/math-academy-id "q-124215" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=8u-6v+2,\\,y=4u-3v-1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=1$\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8u-6v+2 \\\\ 4u-3v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8u-6v \\\\ 4u-3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|=(-3)\\cdot 8-(-6)\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-124215/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124215"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124213/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b136b46c-df89-553f-aca1-8b60d25acf21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124213/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "72f9b162-896f-5c58-be89-4945f8a88dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124213/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "64b87fd5-2822-5e8e-bcb7-ab825981aa70" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124213/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "7ae8c53e-69c8-5169-a8b1-739cbb2e959e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124213/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "b3e300fb-a3f3-51d4-842a-4197cdbf86b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124213/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1816234b-c3c2-5357-8d2c-fff2c3d1d3de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124213/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-124213/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-124213/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-124213/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-124213/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-124213/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124213" :question/id #uuid "11d3a486-18fe-5903-b5cd-5eb90c14163f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124213" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-6v-2,\\,y=5v-u+9.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=4$\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-6v-2 \\\\ 5v-u+9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-6v \\\\ -u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=5\\cdot 2-(-6)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-124213/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124213"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124210/selection/5a3a6135cc8487c4eadda3e9ef972adc31af9f743a54b7fb28b83a7930b673a7" :answer/id #uuid "a7599421-2a18-5beb-a436-488f533e00bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124210/selection/ed498c0998abcb6e5238fb53199317e7024c77fcb90acb19d3e2aff6286f5652" :answer/id #uuid "a39441da-29ec-560b-83dc-8f18218ca9b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124210/selection/2a40fee1d6a127e17f1f3d6139c6c49021f48f9b837354f45c3363da723537f0" :answer/id #uuid "0e0f6c23-8c5a-58ec-8eea-2bd9a8ac9f69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124210/selection/f1eac8bf31988c662296e7b40cf0f707c90b46b548136ef96e77a2bad6e47ea4" :answer/id #uuid "2b1ae89c-32ee-59a8-8783-858df0a3e889" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124210/selection/9c74c795ae613953b0600ea2911c6d11f36397b3d151f7eda64b1684176d7dc5" :answer/id #uuid "815cac7c-f3fd-51cc-9d9f-3d2675fb67ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124210/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a0113b-5818-5f93-a5a3-c253e864b429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124210/selection/2a40fee1d6a127e17f1f3d6139c6c49021f48f9b837354f45c3363da723537f0" :answer-field/choices ["q-124210/selection/5a3a6135cc8487c4eadda3e9ef972adc31af9f743a54b7fb28b83a7930b673a7" "q-124210/selection/ed498c0998abcb6e5238fb53199317e7024c77fcb90acb19d3e2aff6286f5652" "q-124210/selection/2a40fee1d6a127e17f1f3d6139c6c49021f48f9b837354f45c3363da723537f0" "q-124210/selection/f1eac8bf31988c662296e7b40cf0f707c90b46b548136ef96e77a2bad6e47ea4" "q-124210/selection/9c74c795ae613953b0600ea2911c6d11f36397b3d151f7eda64b1684176d7dc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124210" :question/id #uuid "7c4490d1-fbae-525e-81b6-0d9b1dd1e143" :question/math-academy-id "q-124210" :question/problem "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5u-2v-5 \\\\ 10u-4v+1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u-v-4 \\\\ u+5v+7\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u+4v-2 \\\\ 3u+6v+3\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-2v-5 \\\\ 10u-4v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-2v \\\\ 10u-4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}|=-4\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot 10=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v-4 \\\\ u+5v+7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=5\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1=6\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+4v-2 \\\\ 3u+6v+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+4v \\\\ 3u+6v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}|=6\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{2}$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124210/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124210"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124219/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "d3945687-d97e-5f7e-894e-1ec7f2444bb0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124219/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "eb2f2ad0-6d33-534d-9a3a-4a8d49631129" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124219/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "8be9905b-1f4d-568a-bfe0-ae38b3ddcd5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124219/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2b7fe9fa-d6d6-5542-9e3b-97e770f792c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124219/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "d788609a-5bc0-515d-b00e-7fb366c70e9b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124219/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "308e64c4-063b-5783-8c9f-dea135b55caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124219/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-124219/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-124219/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-124219/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-124219/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-124219/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124219" :question/id #uuid "1cec851f-4e5c-5c64-b643-ac79c3379015" :question/math-academy-id "q-124219" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+2v-5,\\,y=2u+v+6.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+2v-5 \\\\ 2u+v+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)=0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-124219/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124219"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-124111"] :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-124244/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "fe77de73-0df8-5db0-a58f-e5cf33a56f99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124244/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "7e5b5664-96a2-5f08-bd47-65c462ef89c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124244/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "4f89f49c-fce6-54d7-9c36-cb0720c1f9d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124244/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "578723df-c35f-505d-9fc7-b134846750a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124244/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "b9f5134d-09f0-53d8-b486-4ba4aeee5be2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124244/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b954e27-e954-55c6-bb7f-cadc956a4ffd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124244/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-124244/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-124244/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-124244/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-124244/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-124244/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124244" :question/id #uuid "d107df60-fa13-547e-9d14-3fa9552fdb97" :question/math-academy-id "q-124244" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 \\\\ b & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+(-1)+8=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124244/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124244"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124228/selection/283bc09f1a097ae8400558511ca45f1013e17c797fdb7407be52694190fd744b" :answer/id #uuid "f36bc9eb-ff82-538a-ad14-64404dd9f3a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124228/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "91f13d9d-08d5-5ca9-a9a1-9eb32d379e32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124228/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "39999165-5c73-5eaa-9538-7c89e9fa9833" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124228/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "c1b267d6-d536-5fea-be96-d10ae5deb102" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124228/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer/id #uuid "62f43d90-7bc1-55ad-a8df-02cadfd589fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124228/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "32dc5950-927a-521f-bc11-5b504e367050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124228/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-124228/selection/283bc09f1a097ae8400558511ca45f1013e17c797fdb7407be52694190fd744b" "q-124228/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-124228/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-124228/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" "q-124228/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124228" :question/id #uuid "c77e304e-2308-55f3-9ac6-6bff36f2165e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124228" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & -2 \\\\ -4 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-7+(-1)+3=-5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124228/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124228"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124241/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "c9e21a12-9817-55e2-9410-15760af09850" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124241/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "bdf7e077-4ce1-5ac5-b4d6-5faf89ca0a5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124241/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "fb02a222-d216-5422-9d96-79eb327a77d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124241/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "bead1787-e564-5cea-b96f-96fc0e77295f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124241/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7f6e23c4-2f20-55b4-b087-e4c396295b73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124241/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f64118b5-dc3c-5d97-a36b-2fb742fa1c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124241/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-124241/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-124241/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-124241/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-124241/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-124241/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124241" :question/id #uuid "13226a12-3e32-5c71-bf59-aa8453555278" :question/math-academy-id "q-124241" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ -1 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 8-9\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{5}{4}+(-\\frac{9}{4})+2=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124241/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124241"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-124249"] :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 \\\\ b & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7-(-5)\\cdot (4)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+4+3=10.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-124233/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "1d44551d-f0cd-5df6-bb36-921143f55039" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124233/selection/35f5572ccca3a8c38d3ca06e2a92b51065f48e45d29833e19f4eb153cae096fc" :answer/id #uuid "d964150c-30e3-5d32-b023-b7ce0ffc42a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124233/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "8ec26753-de81-5ce2-b14c-1d0091dcea0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124233/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "f1b7b124-b323-5a4b-98fb-de8ede3d768f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124233/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "66ba9bc0-eb13-564f-a60d-9ad179ec0468" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124233/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e2f87c4-b833-5e5e-ba3e-37b113445ffd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124233/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-124233/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-124233/selection/35f5572ccca3a8c38d3ca06e2a92b51065f48e45d29833e19f4eb153cae096fc" "q-124233/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-124233/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-124233/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124233" :question/id #uuid "411a2ce2-d777-522f-85f3-9f14ec22afe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124233" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & -1 \\\\ b & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-4)-2\\cdot (-5)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+\\frac{5}{2}+(-2)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124233/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124233"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124276/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "fa590673-e6ec-52c7-b9f3-6c9f619fcad5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124276/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "a011ace9-ca98-5202-958d-cb2b17c3b65a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124276/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "4ded0648-9637-54b0-b9fe-0f9084aab79b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124276/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "02f386d5-b370-563f-9628-226459ab50e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124276/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "6e95a10d-937b-5925-bb96-ca6ff86ea5d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124276/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6504d11e-c740-5e5b-9712-390ac72b3c95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124276/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-124276/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-124276/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-124276/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-124276/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-124276/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124276" :question/id #uuid "c15c122f-0cee-5a43-8b4f-a7fc99f191b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124276" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+7v+2,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-7y+3,\\,v=by+cx-2,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+7v+2 \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+7v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x-2)+(-7)\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (x-2)+4\\cdot (y-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-7y+3 \\\\ 4y-x-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-7y+3,\\,v=4y+(-1)x-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+4+(-1)=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-124276/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124276"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124280/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "c86eb5a0-2108-5dd2-87f3-fb1da76a3517" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124280/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "2f19ae85-7ac6-5e35-a2b6-ab9e8e1a8456" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124280/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "32e6f3a4-35fc-59df-b4e0-f682f79073da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124280/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "d2a425dc-549f-5437-a213-6c494f51a6a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124280/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "a3b1f61f-c6be-56e5-9b70-3993e6d12977" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124280/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a22d0752-22ed-5578-84e6-602fbe6b4f97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124280/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer-field/choices ["q-124280/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-124280/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-124280/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-124280/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-124280/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124280" :question/id #uuid "c3552aa4-679b-5224-86cd-51d6fb797212" :question/math-academy-id "q-124280" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2-u-v,\\,y=3u+4v-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=7+ax-y,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7-4x-y \\\\ 3x+y-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7+(-4)x-y,\\,v=3x+y+(-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+3+(-5)=-6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-124280/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124280"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-124317"] :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=5v-7u+3,\\,y=7v-10u+5.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-5y+4,\\,v=bx+cy+5,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5v-7u+3 \\\\ 7v-10u+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7u+5v \\\\ -10u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot (-7)-5\\cdot (-10)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-3 \\\\ y-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot (x-3)+(-5)\\cdot (y-5) \\\\ 10\\cdot (x-3)+(-7)\\cdot (y-5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7x-5y+4 \\\\ 10x-7y+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7x-5y+4,\\,v=10x+(-7)y+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=7+10+(-7)=10.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-124312/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "03cea75a-2b48-533d-8a09-b88157e6dee2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124312/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "c335e2ba-f6b0-5f9e-af2a-163782e0d2e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124312/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "20d1c890-5088-5604-9749-8fec731b72ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124312/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "e81d8237-12c8-573f-a3f3-41b61f0f167b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124312/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "54c40a29-cda9-5187-ab83-994972d8a532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124312/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2203eddd-9493-50de-8490-c775c45d2eda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124312/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-124312/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-124312/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-124312/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-124312/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-124312/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124312" :question/id #uuid "d9f195c0-9862-5d56-a2f8-0cd7f911970a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124312" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-3v-4,\\,y=2u-4v-5.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+ay+b,\\,v=x-y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v-4 \\\\ 2u-4v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v \\\\ 2u-4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-4\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+4 \\\\ y+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x+4)+(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (y+5) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+4)+(-1)\\cdot (y+5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{3}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-y-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+(-\\frac{3}{2})y+\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=x-y+(-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+(-1)=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-124312/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124312"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124314/selection/cedce138738816f1e959edfd5049bf0c6bcf0acc69b898c7f84479cb3a782ce3" :answer/id #uuid "ad0aa7ad-4b40-5d93-af16-0d946084a4d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124314/selection/34219f229a95b374101226ed6e8380124ab0bbcf04f479caa9d1b203ca15969e" :answer/id #uuid "722c53d5-36ff-506d-b554-2ddf30ae1afc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124314/selection/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818" :answer/id #uuid "1c2a9948-ac27-5356-ad60-dd3e7defa7a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124314/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "f1500fc0-c954-50af-91a8-19bb95eee5c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124314/selection/f7e51073a8044d706f62a310ce4d8f162b2fb8c60dce078b4b3b822d9f8ed6fa" :answer/id #uuid "4380a0cb-1cd8-5fa5-9a9d-eac0eebe39df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124314/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "65896239-c279-5eea-bd7b-fecc4e226ab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124314/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer-field/choices ["q-124314/selection/cedce138738816f1e959edfd5049bf0c6bcf0acc69b898c7f84479cb3a782ce3" "q-124314/selection/34219f229a95b374101226ed6e8380124ab0bbcf04f479caa9d1b203ca15969e" "q-124314/selection/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818" "q-124314/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" "q-124314/selection/f7e51073a8044d706f62a310ce4d8f162b2fb8c60dce078b4b3b822d9f8ed6fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124314" :question/id #uuid "a16fabd1-386c-542d-8938-52c582128a39" :question/math-academy-id "q-124314" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-3v+2,\\,y=6v-3u-4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+a,\\,v=x+by+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v+2 \\\\ 6v-3u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v \\\\ -3u+6v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x-2)+(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (y+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+y \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+0,\\,v=x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-124314/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124314"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-162151"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-162214/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" :answer/id #uuid "4e357246-e39f-5b43-a389-32347e6b5d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162214/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" :answer/id #uuid "8bb0ce14-9eb9-5fdd-9423-99b0c83ce3af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A line segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162214/selection/8898f8c07da7db97f85cb58d6925b060a0f2f97ddf8810b9c3cea168cc03141b" :answer/id #uuid "26eeb1cb-ed34-51fb-9ec5-0fd901d4fcb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A tetrahedron" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162214/selection/efff15d31c09e1f108daa7793309521088dfa694bb5914dcf15d35cd6340a394" :answer/id #uuid "ed1a3a89-105e-5c7c-9837-354046ef445c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162214/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" :answer/id #uuid "3c818c6c-942f-5155-8aba-5ae4aaf196f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162214/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6fd46968-f89f-5b28-9d4f-441583a1f663" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162214/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" :answer-field/choices ["q-162214/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" "q-162214/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" "q-162214/selection/8898f8c07da7db97f85cb58d6925b060a0f2f97ddf8810b9c3cea168cc03141b" "q-162214/selection/efff15d31c09e1f108daa7793309521088dfa694bb5914dcf15d35cd6340a394" "q-162214/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162214" :question/id #uuid "ffabbedd-0576-59b3-b4ce-c62c4314b183" :question/math-academy-id "q-162214" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/00151dc7a58722a649a52494434950695a27cb4e7cad14f32f76abbddf629a91.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u-3v+2,\\,y=4v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-3v+2 \\\\ 4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-3v \\\\ 4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a triangle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162214/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162214"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162225/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" :answer/id #uuid "d43eb5ff-ce1f-5a77-a2d1-a73ad244eb60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A line segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162225/selection/a46b9d9cb098f30c2bfb1712d5eac34a9a6a7683788497e7234aec27f048077a" :answer/id #uuid "7133dcf5-7c00-5315-ae40-1110ac46f71e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A pentagon" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162225/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" :answer/id #uuid "32313f28-8482-502e-bfcd-10e0329c270e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162225/selection/efff15d31c09e1f108daa7793309521088dfa694bb5914dcf15d35cd6340a394" :answer/id #uuid "da0a57b7-f7fa-536c-95c0-bfcc414dd62e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162225/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" :answer/id #uuid "2f7f09c4-a597-54f1-b787-902dd97d6284" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162225/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a46acf57-a601-5b22-8424-fd1454ffc34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162225/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" :answer-field/choices ["q-162225/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" "q-162225/selection/a46b9d9cb098f30c2bfb1712d5eac34a9a6a7683788497e7234aec27f048077a" "q-162225/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" "q-162225/selection/efff15d31c09e1f108daa7793309521088dfa694bb5914dcf15d35cd6340a394" "q-162225/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162225" :question/id #uuid "b88904fb-4625-5860-ba0f-4152001e44ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162225" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/afee2a3c30384baa044088177550b0ac5ed2de228b14e48dd7f729e8a7493318.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a line segment." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162225/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162225"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162224/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" :answer/id #uuid "510597d2-409d-5b17-9892-6212e852891a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162224/selection/04a22f057dfb78cf7268b7e28949d343c0e04babc5e92513e1ecaee0279bd0e7" :answer/id #uuid "764a4843-cb3c-5056-a030-d4a37bd17bd0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162224/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" :answer/id #uuid "798b6c8e-353f-5085-9ea9-4f204689eb3c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162224/selection/001baee294998436b951ad420f7c3baae45fc747ccaa216f65a68c68d0205dfa" :answer/id #uuid "35e7a352-b7cc-519c-b8f8-b0ef51db98ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A cube" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162224/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" :answer/id #uuid "0ba928c8-158b-555c-9a93-f7d0a4941d6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A line segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162224/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "99ffbdea-9bd2-51b4-8d4d-49c9c92439f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162224/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" :answer-field/choices ["q-162224/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" "q-162224/selection/04a22f057dfb78cf7268b7e28949d343c0e04babc5e92513e1ecaee0279bd0e7" "q-162224/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" "q-162224/selection/001baee294998436b951ad420f7c3baae45fc747ccaa216f65a68c68d0205dfa" "q-162224/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162224" :question/id #uuid "04802bc7-899d-54a2-8b0c-e29ee38e1277" :question/math-academy-id "q-162224" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/b57fe5ce4b75c7daa5c866f3eb537ed62546e5fced67f6e2cb82af768cf382cb.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is a zero matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a point on the plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162224/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162224"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162222/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" :answer/id #uuid "0cc3ab06-7358-5336-b82c-aa1100b4d991" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162222/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" :answer/id #uuid "ac34f495-61f5-5768-82d4-4e639e1fd609" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A triangle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162222/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" :answer/id #uuid "49f56160-72b5-5e27-8423-80735ee0911b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A line segment" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162222/selection/17289e1e8745422c797709f99e0edd5559d7531dceba3bb829f55cda3b5df310" :answer/id #uuid "38742691-7b93-5a94-be7c-498226e8c7e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A square" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162222/selection/04a22f057dfb78cf7268b7e28949d343c0e04babc5e92513e1ecaee0279bd0e7" :answer/id #uuid "3d3853d5-ea46-535b-8b6a-042b00eef759" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162222/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "01d1a3dc-4d87-5abc-aaae-33b52479c568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162222/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" :answer-field/choices ["q-162222/selection/4e58580515bb45c045fd2f14cf2873f6efa6f0e2170ad5b45d216042e4ee999a" "q-162222/selection/99d5aeb2f87bf6ef13f130429773b8288f79a42bed11813730ac0875b5e7a025" "q-162222/selection/c2d1a5ac4ed1170ec4ba684e8dd01cdba007525d6370ddfd38066ec585122318" "q-162222/selection/17289e1e8745422c797709f99e0edd5559d7531dceba3bb829f55cda3b5df310" "q-162222/selection/04a22f057dfb78cf7268b7e28949d343c0e04babc5e92513e1ecaee0279bd0e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162222" :question/id #uuid "32de6dc3-b87c-5a15-9ab4-dd7618d403da" :question/math-academy-id "q-162222" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/957d55e3fb66bb2633798cf13d5c6049ffa4524ca585515ffaf88771ee32fe9b.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is a zero matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a point on the plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162222/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162222"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12428"] :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v - 2, \\qquad y = v - u + 5.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix representing the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v-2 \\\\ v-u+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\det(A) = 0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12429"] :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $\\mathbf T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 7-(-9)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = -7 + 3 + 8 = 4.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12430"] :question/problem "The affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v + 2, \\qquad y = 4u - 5v + 9.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ of $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\nu = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}x - y + 1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+2 \\\\ 4u-5v+9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ 4u-5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-5)-(-1)\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y-9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y-9) \\\\ 4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y-9)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5x-y-1 \\\\ 4x-y+1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{5}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{(-1)}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{4}x - y + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 5 + (-1) + 4 = 8.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12441"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/8797a50ddc0750a97190f96d8bc1cffe70c6ef9cc8e55a46f5a75d6b6702c81d.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nDescribe the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
